You keep two lists for a volunteer group: the notes from meetings
and the current handoff. On a busy morning you want to find each
mention of the library, then see what changed in the handoff. You
don't need an app that manages the group. You need a short routine
whose inputs are known, whose results you can explain, and whose
mistakes leave you with the original notes.

\begin{objectives}
\item Search two notes by a literal phrase and locate the matching lines.
\item Compare two versions without changing them.
\item Write a repeatable read-only routine with useful error and
no-result messages.
\end{objectives}

\section{Give the routine a home}\label{sec:46-setup}

A daily routine is easier to trust when it has a boundary. Decide
which files belong to the routine, what success looks like, and who
will act on the result. Make the first run small enough to check by
reading every line. If its answers are wrong on two notes, a faster
search across a hundred notes does not make it more useful.

Use a spare terminal and POSIX shell syntax. If your usual shell
requires different syntax, start a child with \cmd|sh| first. The
following setup makes a fresh directory; stop if the assignment
or \cmd|cd| fails and don't type the writes elsewhere. Local
\texttt{notes}, \texttt{before.txt}, and \texttt{after.txt} are
fixture names, not an existing project. The redirections replace
their destination files if the commands are run again.

\begin{terminal}
lab=$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/utp46.XXXXXX") && cd "$lab"
pwd
mkdir ./notes
printf '%s\n' 'Library: ask for the key' 'Hall: confirm chairs' \
    > ./notes/monday.txt
printf '%s\n' 'Riverside library: opens at noon' \
    > ./notes/thursday.txt
\end{terminal}

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' 'Start at ten' 'Library: ask for the key' \
    > ./before.txt
printf '%s\n' 'Start at noon' 'Library: ask for the key' \
    > ./after.txt
\end{terminal}

The names show which file is the earlier version. In real work,
date labels or a predictable exported name are often better, as
long as the team agrees on the order. An editor's modified date
is not a good substitute for knowing which file the team reviewed.
Copying a file may affect dates; two files can also have identical
times but different contents. The routine compares text, not
timestamps or changes still unsaved in your editor.

\input{figures/chapter-46/routine.tikz}

Figure~\ref{fig:46-routine} separates reading, interpreting, and
deciding. The report should not quietly make the decision for
you. For example the start time changed, but the command cannot
tell whether the change was approved. Keep the step that merges
or forwards the handoff separate from the step that examines it.
That separation gives you a stopping point if the search turns
up unfamiliar text or the comparison has the order backwards.

\section{Find evidence you can return to}\label{sec:46-search}

Use a literal phrase for the first search. \cmd|-F| treats the
pattern as a fixed string, \cmd|-i| ignores differences in case,
and \cmd|-n| reports line numbers. \cmd|-e| identifies the
next argument as the pattern, which matters if you later search
for text beginning with a hyphen. Search explicit paths instead
of every file in your home directory. With two filename operands
and \cmd|-n|, the output identifies which file contains each
line. A teammate can go straight to the finding without trusting
that your terminal scrollback is saved.
\IndexTerm{workflow!notes search}\IndexCommand{grep}

\begin{terminal}
grep -Fin -e 'library' ./notes/monday.txt ./notes/thursday.txt
printf 'search status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

\begin{terminaloutput}
./notes/monday.txt:1:Library: ask for the key
./notes/thursday.txt:1:Riverside library: opens at noon
search status: 0
\end{terminaloutput}

The matched text is not changed to lowercase. \cmd|-i| affects
matching; the contents are still printed as stored. The search
does not prove the notes are about the same library. The
relationship is a reading task. It also doesn't find alternative
names unless the note contains the literal word you chose. Case
folding depends on the locale, especially away from the simple
English letters used here. Verify a multilingual search on known
examples before calling it complete (Chapter~\ref{ch:20}).

When a search appears to miss a result, first read a known
source line and check the phrase and files you selected. Try
a narrower read instead of changing the search to an unbounded
recursive one. Recursive searches can reach unrelated text,
archives, or private data and can produce valid-looking matches
from the wrong project.

\begin{terminal}
head -n 1 ./notes/thursday.txt
grep -Fin -e 'bookshop' ./notes/monday.txt ./notes/thursday.txt
printf 'search status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

Expect one line of preview, then no match line for the search
and a status of one. If either input is missing or unreadable,
do not label it an ordinary empty result. For this non-quiet
search, an error returns greater than one even though some
matches may already have been printed. This matters when deciding
whether the routine checked both notes.

\input{figures/chapter-46/search.tikz}

Figure~\ref{fig:46-search} relates the result to the source file.
The output is an aid for human review, not a universal machine
record. A filename could itself have a colon or even a newline,
whereas our explicit fixture names do not. Avoid splitting
arbitrary \cmd|grep| output on colon and treating the pieces as
a trustworthy table. When you need structured fields, make a
purpose-built data source (Chapter~\ref{ch:24}).

\section{Read a change in the right direction}\label{sec:46-diff}

Use unified \cmd|diff| with the older file first. It prints
header lines for each file and hunk headers that locate the
changed region; date fields may vary. In the body a leading
minus labels a line in the first file and a leading plus
labels a line in the second. A leading space gives unchanged
context. These markers are not part of the handoff. The
commands below only read the inputs.
\IndexCommand{diff}\IndexTerm{version comparison}

\begin{terminal}
diff -u ./before.txt ./after.txt
printf 'comparison status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

The meaningful part here is the minus for ``Start at ten''
and the plus for ``Start at noon''. Expect the unchanged
library instruction too, because it sits next to the change.
Status one says the comparison found a difference, so a script
that handles all nonzero statuses as failures would hide the
answer you wanted. Status greater than one is the branch that
requires investigation. If a change looks backwards, check the
operand order before sending it to anyone.

\begin{quicktable}{lYY}
\toprule
Status & Search with \texttt{grep} & Comparison with \texttt{diff} \\
\midrule
0 & At least one match & No differences \\
1 & No matches & Differences found \\
Greater than 1 & Error reading or applying the search & Error comparing the inputs \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

If you need a copy for discussion, save just this comparison into
a separate file. The output filename is new and dedicated to the
routine. Redirection opens it before \cmd|diff| starts; the
presence of the file does not establish success. Do not make
either input path the output.

\begin{terminal}
diff -u ./before.txt ./after.txt > ./changes.txt
printf 'comparison status: %s\n' "$?"
head -n 8 ./changes.txt
\end{terminal}

This short preview should include the change. Treat saved
comparisons as potentially sensitive: unified output generally
shows unchanged lines next to what changed. Redact or paraphrase
the harmless portion of a real report before sharing. Don't
paste a full diff into a public channel just because it is short.

\input{figures/chapter-46/changes.tikz}

\begin{dialoguebox}{A successful difference}
\Brian{The comparison returned one. Didn't we just call that a failure?}
\Stephen{We called nonzero worth reading. This tool uses one to say the files differ.}
\Brian{So we should treat it as success every time?}
\Stephen{Not if we expected the copies to be identical. The result can be correct and still tell us not to proceed.}
\Brian{And a difference isn't the same as a file the tool couldn't read.}
\Stephen{Right. Give those outcomes different messages in the routine.}
\Brian{Then I can decide from the message, not the number alone.}
\DialogueConclusion{Interpret status in the context of the task.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\section{Keep the repeatable part small}

A script is justified when you repeat the check and want the
same source files and status handling each time. Use the copy in
\FilePath{listings/chapter-46/check-notes.sh}, or save the text
below as \texttt{check-notes.sh} in the practice directory using
your editor. Do not try to enter the entire listing as commands:
it contains \cmd|exit| for the script. Use \cmd|sh ./check-notes.sh|
to run it without changing the file mode. It reads fixed
relative paths, so verify you are in the intended directory.
The braces with \cmd|:-| pick the word \texttt{library} if the
first argument is missing or empty. Quoting preserves a phrase
containing spaces as one pattern. The cases interpret the
previous command's status immediately; errors leave the script
with a nonzero status. Matches and differences are useful
answers, not execution errors.

\ShellFile{listings/chapter-46/check-notes.sh}

\begin{terminal}
sh -n ./check-notes.sh
sh ./check-notes.sh 'library'
sh ./check-notes.sh 'bookshop'
\end{terminal}

Expect the same comparison in both runs; the second prints a
no-match message for the notes. \cmd|sh -n| checks syntax, not
the validity of the inputs. If \cmd|sh -n| fails, correct the
file before continuing. Test a typical match and a deliberate
miss before trusting the shortcut. The headings begin on their
own lines so a spoken listener can tell which operation came
next. A batch job that needs to parse the match data should
instead separate report headings from data rows.

\begin{quicktable}{p{.26\linewidth}Y}
\toprule
When & Condition for proceeding \\
\midrule
Before running & The working directory and fixture names are
correct; both handoffs are saved and their order is known. \\
While reading & Match lines identify the source; differences
use the expected older-to-newer order. \\
Before sharing & A person has interpreted the findings and
screened the text for private information. \\
Next time & If a pathname changes, update the routine deliberately
and repeat the small tests before trusting a larger search. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\begin{takeaways}
\item Write down the specific questions a daily routine answers.
\item Keep searches and comparisons read-only until you understand the result.
\item Handle no matches, differences, and execution errors as distinct outcomes.
\end{takeaways}

\begin{exercise}{A phrase with spaces}\label{ex:46-phrase}
Find the literal phrase ``opens at'' in the fixture notes.
Run the routine with that phrase as one argument. No extra
files are needed.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
sh ./check-notes.sh 'opens at'
\end{terminal}
Expect only the Thursday line in the search section and the
same handoff comparison as before. The quotes keep the phrase in
one argument; without them only the first word would be used.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Read a reversed comparison}\label{ex:46-reverse}
Run a comparison with the new file first and the old file
second. Locate the unchanged line and explain which change
markers swapped. Do not save the output over either handoff.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
diff -u ./after.txt ./before.txt
\end{terminal}
``Start at noon'' is now marked with a minus and ``Start at
ten'' with a plus. The library instruction is still context.
The exit status is still one.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Check what a missing input does}\label{ex:46-missing}
Temporarily rename the Thursday fixture, run the routine, and
capture its exit status immediately. Restore the filename
before cleaning up. The replacement name is unused in the fresh
fixture; \texttt{mv -i} asks before overwriting if a conflict
does exist. Cancel rather than overwriting.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
mv -i ./notes/thursday.txt ./notes/thursday.hold
sh ./check-notes.sh
printf 'routine status: %s\n' "$?"
mv -i ./notes/thursday.hold ./notes/thursday.txt
\end{terminal}
Expect a reading diagnostic and the script's error message;
Monday's match may also appear. The comparison is not attempted.
The routine exits with status two. Restore before the usual
cleanup even if the output surprised you.
\end{solution}

Confirm the fixture root with \cmd|pwd|. Remove only the
fixture names; omit \texttt{check-notes.sh} or \texttt{changes.txt}
if you did not create them. Then remove the empty directories.

\begin{terminal}
rm ./notes/monday.txt ./notes/thursday.txt
rm ./before.txt ./after.txt ./changes.txt ./check-notes.sh
rmdir ./notes
cd / && rmdir "$lab"
\end{terminal}

